    "text": "A1 is \"Cannot tell\": the seven-day search was evaluated only from two hours before publication and is partial, so earlier misuse in the unevaluated window can't be ruled out.\n\nA2 is \"No\": the link between the payout destination and the two other accounts was learned a day after publication, so it was not among the inputs the check had when it scored the account.\n\nA3 is \"No, not by itself\": a shared payout destination is consistent with one owner but does not establish it.\n\nA4 is \"No\": no reporting window is defined for the victim-report or incident channels, so silence after 30 days carries no information about legitimacy.\n\nA5 is \"Yes\": a change of region and fingerprint, plus a new webhook, a payout-destination change and a faster payout schedule within minutes, is a clear anomaly.\n\nB1 is \"No\": the two chargebacks recorded at account X are linked to D's transactions, so D's chargeback count is effectively 2, the same as A, B and C. On the chargeback-or-alert measure, D (2 of 100) is also equal to C and below A and B, but not lower than \"the others\" as a whole.\n\nB2 is \"No\": the 5 percent is a pooled figure, and account A alone is at 8 of 100, which is above 5 percent.\n\nB3 is \"No\": the search was sampled at 10 percent, hit its row limit and was truncated, so it cannot show that no other accounts exist.\n\nB4 is \"No\": both analyst notes link to the same cluster assessment that was open in the case, so they are not independent confirmations.\n\nB5 is \"No\": the blocked payment closed the chargeback channel for that payment, so the absence of a chargeback says nothing about whether it was fraud.\n\nB6 is \"Yes\": shared templates and a descriptor pattern across eleven storefronts that settle through the four accounts justify investigating them together.\n\nRESULT CH-055 | A1: Cannot tell | A2: No | A3: No, not by itself | A4: No | A5: Yes | B1: No | B2: No | B3: No | B4: No | B5: No | B6: Yes"
